exercises.problem_statement:
Review this API controller pattern, repeated across a dozen actions in your project:

```csharp
[HttpPut("{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Put(int id, [FromBody]Author author)
{
    if ((await _authorRepository.ListAsync()).All(a => a.Id != id))
    {
        return NotFound(id);
    }
    if (!ModelState.IsValid)
    {
        return BadRequest(ModelState);
    }
    author.Id = id;
    await _authorRepository.UpdateAsync(author);
    return Ok();
}
```

Additionally, one service in the project calls `AuditLogger.WriteStatic(...)`, a static method that writes to the database. Identify the cross-cutting concerns duplicated in the actions, show what the action looks like after refactoring with the recommended ASP.NET Core mechanism, and explain what is wrong with the static call.

exercises.expected_solution:
The cross-cutting concerns are model validation (return BadRequest when validation fails) and existence checking (return NotFound for keys that don't exist) — common error-handling policies cluttering every action. Pull them into MVC filters, which run code before or after steps in the request pipeline and are applied as attributes at action, controller, or global level. A validation filter:

```csharp
public class ValidateModelAttribute : ActionFilterAttribute
{
    public override void OnActionExecuting(ActionExecutingContext context)
    {
        if (!context.ModelState.IsValid)
        {
            context.Result = new BadRequestObjectResult(context.ModelState);
        }
    }
}
```

(For APIs, the `[ApiController]` attribute enforces this behavior without a separate filter.) A `[ValidateAuthorExists]` filter likewise checks the record and returns 404 before the action executes. The refactored action becomes extremely thin:

```csharp
[HttpPut("{id}")]
[ValidateAuthorExists]
public async Task<IActionResult> Put(int id, [FromBody]Author author)
{
    await _authorRepository.UpdateAsync(author);
    return Ok();
}
```

The static call is the 'static cling' code smell: the method calls a static method that writes to a database, so it is tightly coupled to that infrastructure — anything that breaks the database call breaks the method, and testing requires either commercial mocking libraries to mock statics or a real test database. Following the Explicit Dependencies Principle, the service should request an abstraction (e.g., an injected logger/auditing interface) through its constructor rather than calling the static implementation directly.

(a) Role restriction:

```csharp
[Authorize(Roles = "HRManager,Finance")]
public class SalaryController : Controller { }
```

Users in either role get access; to require multiple roles, apply the attribute multiple times. (b) Claims policy — configure once, apply everywhere:

```csharp
services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("EmployeeOnly", policy => policy.RequireClaim("EmployeeNumber"));
});
```

then `[Authorize(Policy = "EmployeeOnly")]` on the relevant pages/actions. Policies separate the kind of action being restricted from the specific roles/rules, so adding a new role later means updating one policy instead of every [Authorize] role list (at minimum, replace repeated role strings with constants). (c) Cookie schemes use redirection to login pages, which suits browser-based web apps but not APIs: API clients are code (e.g., HttpClient) expecting a usable response or a status code — they don't render HTML, so redirecting them to a login page is inappropriate. Instead, most web APIs should use token-based authentication (typically JWT bearer tokens), which is stateless and designed to be scalable: configure `AddAuthentication` with `JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme` and `AddJwtBearer` in the API project (keeping the API as a separate project also keeps a single default scheme per project). The Blazor WebAssembly client authenticates with the auth provider, stores the issued token, and adds it to every API call as a header: `new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", token)`. Also remember all authentication and authorization checks must be performed on the server, since users can bypass the Blazor app and call the APIs directly, and `UseAuthentication`/`UseAuthorization` must appear before `MapRazorPages`.

Encapsulate caching in its own class instead of putting it in data access or UI logic: create a CachedCatalogService that implements ICatalogService using the Proxy (or Decorator) pattern, taking IMemoryCache and the concrete CatalogService in its constructor. Each method uses `_cache.GetOrCreateAsync(key, ...)` with a SlidingExpiration (e.g., a short default like 30–60 seconds) and falls through to the underlying CatalogService on a miss. The paged items key must encode all the query parameters, e.g. `string cacheKey = $"items-{pageIndex}-{itemsPage}-{brandID}-{typeId}";`, while brands and types use fixed keys. Register in Program.cs:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddMemoryCache();
builder.Services.AddScoped<ICatalogService, CachedCatalogService>();
builder.Services.AddScoped<CatalogService>();
```

so consumers get the cached version while the wrapper still receives the real CatalogService. Stale data handling: keep durations small — a page requested 10 times per second cached for one minute cuts queries from 600 to 1 per minute (99.8% reduction); extending to an hour gains little (99.997%) while dramatically increasing the likelihood and age of stale data. On updates, proactively remove entries: call `_cache.Remove(cacheKey)` in the update code when the key is known; when many entries depend on the same data (e.g., everything brand-related), create dependencies between entries with a CancellationChangeToken stored in the cache and expire them all at once by canceling the token. Finally, measure data access and page performance before applying caching — it consumes server memory and adds complexity, so don't prematurely optimize.
